NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"ready-callback-writes-during-a-truncate","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ready-callback writes during a truncate"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Base revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"931e8346f"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":"). Grid revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"4c338ddbd"},{"type":"text","value":". First\nrepair revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"79c7786b4"},{"type":"text","value":" (deferral). Split-transition revision:\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"9bbeb1063"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"law","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Law"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each change message must be valid for a consumer that has applied every\nearlier message: an insert names an absent key, and an update or delete names\na present one. Authority: the change-message contract in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/change-event-history-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and issue #1901."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A truncate commit that makes the Collection ready runs ready callbacks\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFirstReady"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status:change"},{"type":"text","value":" listeners) during the commit, and a\ncallback may write. The truncate's messages and the callback's messages\ntogether must be valid for every subscriber, with or without initial state and\nwith or without a filter. Each subscriber must end at the Collection's rows.\nSubscribers receive the truncate's messages only after the Collection is ready."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"gap-and-bug","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap and bug"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State mutation round 3 found that a mutant which ran "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markReady"},{"type":"text","value":" before the\ntruncate reapply survived the suite. The ready-callback witness covered only an\nedit of a replaced key, through a subscriber with initial state, whose sent-key\nfilter hides a repeated message."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The bug on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":": the truncate built its batch from the replaced rows, then\ncalled "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markReady"},{"type":"text","value":" before it published. A callback's messages describe the\nreplaced rows, but they reached subscribers before the batch that moves\nsubscribers to those rows. Reachable failures:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A callback deletes a replaced key. The callback publishes the delete, and\nthen the prefix deletes the key again."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A callback deletes a key that only the replacement holds. The subscriber\ngets a delete for a row it never held."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A callback changes a key that has an active prior request. The batch's\nre-applied insert still carries the prior value."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A filtered subscriber holds the pre-truncate row. The callback's delete\ncarries the replaced value, so the filter drops it, and the subscriber keeps\na row that the Collection removed."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first repair ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"7331f25aa"},{"type":"text","value":") dropped prefix deletes for keys a callback\ndeleted. Code review and CodeRabbit showed the other three failures. That\nrepair also made the stale re-applied row silent, because the repeated delete\nno longer exposed it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"repair-and-witnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Repair and witnesses"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ready transition takes a step that runs after the status reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ready"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand before status listeners, ready callbacks, and the empty ready event. A\ntruncate that makes the Collection ready emits its batch in that step. So\nthe batch is built and enriched before any callback runs, subscribers\nreceive it while the Collection is ready, and every ready effect follows it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An earlier revision held the batch in a publication deferral around\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markReady"},{"type":"text","value":". A high-effort review found side effects of that design, listed\nbelow, and the maintainer chose to split the ready transition instead."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The grid in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-sync-reentrancy-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" derives expected\nrows from a model that overlays active intents, in order, on the source rows.\nIt crosses two hooks, four prior requests, every legal callback write to keys\n1 through 4, and three subscribers. Before the truncate the source holds keys\n1 and 4. The replacement holds 1 and 2, so key 4's prefix delete must remain."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each grid case also checks three observations. A message that carries a\nsource row is synced and remote unless a prior request owns its key. A live\nquery over the Collection becomes ready showing replaced rows. A raw\nsubscriber that the ready callback creates receives messages that are valid\nfor the rows it saw."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"high-effort-review-of-the-deferral","children":[{"type":"text","value":"High-effort review of the deferral"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Item"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Finding"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The deferral enriched the batch at publication, so a source row could go out as local."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed by the split. 48 grid cases fail with the deferral."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The empty ready event skipped the deferral, so a live query became ready with pre-truncate rows."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed by the split. All 144 cases fail with the deferral."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A truncate committed inside the sync function, with a throwing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFirstReady"},{"type":"text","value":", throws from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":" and moves the Collection to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ops.markReady()"},{"type":"text","value":" defers the same error."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" too. Open, for a separate fix."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A subscriber that a ready callback creates received the whole batch."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed by the split. 120 cases fail with the deferral."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The deferral delivered the batch and the callbacks' messages as one uncomposed batch."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed by the split: they are separate publications."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The deferral is a truncate-only special case. Split the ready transition."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adopted."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One failure-fidelity case was vacuous."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed: an already-ready Collection runs no ready callbacks, so the case is removed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The grid did not check virtual props, live queries, or callback subscribers."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed: see the witnesses above."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"9"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two guards for the deferral encoded one condition."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gone with the deferral."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A cleanup inside a ready listener dropped the deferred batch."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed by the split: the batch emits before listeners run."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"failure-fidelity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Failure fidelity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit found that the deferral let a subscriber error escape from the\npublication before the commit resolved its applied receipts. A probe showed the\nsame hang on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":", through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markReady"},{"type":"text","value":"'s empty ready event. When a truncate\nmade the Collection ready, a throwing subscriber or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFirstReady"},{"type":"text","value":" callback left\na held receipt pending forever."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The emit and the ready transition now run through one capture that keeps the\nfirst error. The commit reports that error after its receipts settle, as it\ndoes when the Collection is already ready. A witness holds a sync transaction\nbehind a persisting request, then truncates while a subscriber or a ready\ncallback throws. A third case throws from a subscriber on an already-ready\nCollection. The two not-yet-ready cases fail on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" and on the uncaptured\ndeferral."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-outcomes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC outcomes"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The law above names its authority and the subscriber\nmodes it covers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The overlay model reads no production state. The checker\nvalidates each message against the subscriber's own replica."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The prose above the grid states the law, the model, and\nwhy keys 2 and 4 are in the grid."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The enumeration control checks the case count, five named\nwitnesses, and two excluded illegal writes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each case writes through a real Collection's sync and\nmutation APIs. It observes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribeChanges"},{"type":"text","value":" batches, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.status"},{"type":"text","value":"\nat delivery, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.state"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006: met."}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" and the first repair each fail 54 of 144 cases."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A variant that marks the Collection ready after emitting fails 122 cases,\nbecause subscribers then receive the batch while the Collection is loading."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The publication deferral fails all 144 cases on the live-query check, 120\non the callback-subscriber check, and 48 on the virtual-props check."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The grid is a fixed enumeration."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" No stateful model changed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" \"Ready callback\" means an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFirstReady"},{"type":"text","value":" callback or a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status:change"},{"type":"text","value":" listener for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ready"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each case unsubscribes, settles its requests, and cleans\nup the Collection in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":" block. The failure-fidelity witness checks\nthat a thrown subscriber or callback error is reported after every receipt\nsettles."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" No reviewer named a fault shared by production\nand the model."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012: met by this record."}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013: met."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each callback write appears beside its neighbors: the same\nkey with and without a prior request, and a replaced key beside a\nreplacement-only key and an omitted key."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014: not applicable."}]},{"type":"text","value":" No controlled provider or host supplies a\npremise."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A truncate committed inside the sync function itself, whose ready callback\nthrew, threw from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":" and moved the Collection to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":". The\n"},{"type":"link","href":"2026-10-05-round-3-followups.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"follow-up record"}]},{"type":"text","value":" defers that error the way\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ops.markReady()"},{"type":"text","value":" does."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The grid covers one optimistic callback write per truncate. These histories\nremain outside it:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a callback that rolls back an existing request;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a non-optimistic callback write;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"several callbacks that write;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"generated histories that combine ready callbacks with the optimistic-history\ngrammar."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"note-added-2026-10-05-settlement-drop","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note added 2026-10-05: settlement drop"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This fix merged with the settlement-drop change\n("},{"type":"link","href":"2026-10-03-settlement-drop.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2026-10-03 review"}]},{"type":"text","value":"), where readiness counts\naccepted rows. A ready truncate batch still publishes before ready effects\nrun: the reentrancy oracle passes on the merged revision, and S6 still fails\nit."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"ready-callback-writes-during-a-truncate","text":"Ready-callback writes during a truncate","level":1},{"id":"law","text":"Law","level":2},{"id":"gap-and-bug","text":"Gap and bug","level":2},{"id":"repair-and-witnesses","text":"Repair and witnesses","level":2},{"id":"high-effort-review-of-the-deferral","text":"High-effort review of the deferral","level":2},{"id":"failure-fidelity","text":"Failure fidelity","level":2},{"id":"orc-outcomes","text":"ORC outcomes","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2},{"id":"note-added-2026-10-05-settlement-drop","text":"Note added 2026-10-05: settlement drop","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Ready-callback writes during a truncate\n\nBase revision: `931e8346f` (`main`). Grid revision: `4c338ddbd`. First\nrepair revision: `79c7786b4` (deferral). Split-transition revision:\n`9bbeb1063`.\n\n## Law\n\nEach change message must be valid for a consumer that has applied every\nearlier message: an insert names an absent key, and an update or delete names\na present one. Authority: the change-message contract in\n`packages/db/tests/change-event-history-oracle.test.ts` and issue #1901.\n\nA truncate commit that makes the Collection ready runs ready callbacks\n(`onFirstReady` and `status:change` listeners) during the commit, and a\ncallback may write. The truncate's messages and the callback's messages\ntogether must be valid for every subscriber, with or without initial state and\nwith or without a filter. Each subscriber must end at the Collection's rows.\nSubscribers receive the truncate's messages only after the Collection is ready.\n\n## Gap and bug\n\nState mutation round 3 found that a mutant which ran `markReady` before the\ntruncate reapply survived the suite. The ready-callback witness covered only an\nedit of a replaced key, through a subscriber with initial state, whose sent-key\nfilter hides a repeated message.\n\nThe bug on `main`: the truncate built its batch from the replaced rows, then\ncalled `markReady` before it published. A callback's messages describe the\nreplaced rows, but they reached subscribers before the batch that moves\nsubscribers to those rows. Reachable failures:\n\n- A callback deletes a replaced key. The callback publishes the delete, and\n  then the prefix deletes the key again.\n- A callback deletes a key that only the replacement holds. The subscriber\n  gets a delete for a row it never held.\n- A callback changes a key that has an active prior request. The batch's\n  re-applied insert still carries the prior value.\n- A filtered subscriber holds the pre-truncate row. The callback's delete\n  carries the replaced value, so the filter drops it, and the subscriber keeps\n  a row that the Collection removed.\n\nThe first repair (`7331f25aa`) dropped prefix deletes for keys a callback\ndeleted. Code review and CodeRabbit showed the other three failures. That\nrepair also made the stale re-applied row silent, because the repeated delete\nno longer exposed it.\n\n## Repair and witnesses\n\n- The ready transition takes a step that runs after the status reads `ready`\n  and before status listeners, ready callbacks, and the empty ready event. A\n  truncate that makes the Collection ready emits its batch in that step. So\n  the batch is built and enriched before any callback runs, subscribers\n  receive it while the Collection is ready, and every ready effect follows it.\n- An earlier revision held the batch in a publication deferral around\n  `markReady`. A high-effort review found side effects of that design, listed\n  below, and the maintainer chose to split the ready transition instead.\n- The grid in `collection-sync-reentrancy-oracle.test.ts` derives expected\n  rows from a model that overlays active intents, in order, on the source rows.\n  It crosses two hooks, four prior requests, every legal callback write to keys\n  1 through 4, and three subscribers. Before the truncate the source holds keys\n  1 and 4. The replacement holds 1 and 2, so key 4's prefix delete must remain.\n- Each grid case also checks three observations. A message that carries a\n  source row is synced and remote unless a prior request owns its key. A live\n  query over the Collection becomes ready showing replaced rows. A raw\n  subscriber that the ready callback creates receives messages that are valid\n  for the rows it saw.\n\n## High-effort review of the deferral\n\n| Item | Finding | Outcome |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | The deferral enriched the batch at publication, so a source row could go out as local. | Fixed by the split. 48 grid cases fail with the deferral. |\n| 2 | The empty ready event skipped the deferral, so a live query became ready with pre-truncate rows. | Fixed by the split. All 144 cases fail with the deferral. |\n| 3 | A truncate committed inside the sync function, with a throwing `onFirstReady`, throws from `commit()` and moves the Collection to `error`. `ops.markReady()` defers the same error. | Confirmed on `main` too. Open, for a separate fix. |\n| 4 | A subscriber that a ready callback creates received the whole batch. | Fixed by the split. 120 cases fail with the deferral. |\n| 5 | The deferral delivered the batch and the callbacks' messages as one uncomposed batch. | Fixed by the split: they are separate publications. |\n| 6 | The deferral is a truncate-only special case. Split the ready transition. | Adopted. |\n| 7 | One failure-fidelity case was vacuous. | Fixed: an already-ready Collection runs no ready callbacks, so the case is removed. |\n| 8 | The grid did not check virtual props, live queries, or callback subscribers. | Fixed: see the witnesses above. |\n| 9 | The two guards for the deferral encoded one condition. | Gone with the deferral. |\n| 10 | A cleanup inside a ready listener dropped the deferred batch. | Fixed by the split: the batch emits before listeners run. |\n\n## Failure fidelity\n\nCodeRabbit found that the deferral let a subscriber error escape from the\npublication before the commit resolved its applied receipts. A probe showed the\nsame hang on `main`, through `markReady`'s empty ready event. When a truncate\nmade the Collection ready, a throwing subscriber or `onFirstReady` callback left\na held receipt pending forever.\n\nThe emit and the ready transition now run through one capture that keeps the\nfirst error. The commit reports that error after its receipts settle, as it\ndoes when the Collection is already ready. A witness holds a sync transaction\nbehind a persisting request, then truncates while a subscriber or a ready\ncallback throws. A third case throws from a subscriber on an already-ready\nCollection. The two not-yet-ready cases fail on `main` and on the uncaptured\ndeferral.\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\n- **ORC-001: met.** The law above names its authority and the subscriber\n  modes it covers.\n- **ORC-002: met.** The overlay model reads no production state. The checker\n  validates each message against the subscriber's own replica.\n- **ORC-003: met.** The prose above the grid states the law, the model, and\n  why keys 2 and 4 are in the grid.\n- **ORC-004: met.** The enumeration control checks the case count, five named\n  witnesses, and two excluded illegal writes.\n- **ORC-005: met.** Each case writes through a real Collection's sync and\n  mutation APIs. It observes `subscribeChanges` batches, `collection.status`\n  at delivery, and `collection.state`.\n- **ORC-006: met.**\n  - `main` and the first repair each fail 54 of 144 cases.\n  - A variant that marks the Collection ready after emitting fails 122 cases,\n    because subscribers then receive the batch while the Collection is loading.\n  - The publication deferral fails all 144 cases on the live-query check, 120\n    on the callback-subscriber check, and 48 on the virtual-props check.\n- **ORC-007: not applicable.** The grid is a fixed enumeration.\n- **ORC-008: not applicable.** No stateful model changed.\n- **ORC-009: met.** \"Ready callback\" means an `onFirstReady` callback or a\n  `status:change` listener for `ready`.\n- **ORC-010: met.** Each case unsubscribes, settles its requests, and cleans\n  up the Collection in a `finally` block. The failure-fidelity witness checks\n  that a thrown subscriber or callback error is reported after every receipt\n  settles.\n- **ORC-011: not applicable.** No reviewer named a fault shared by production\n  and the model.\n- **ORC-012: met by this record.**\n- **ORC-013: met.** Each callback write appears beside its neighbors: the same\n  key with and without a prior request, and a replaced key beside a\n  replacement-only key and an omitted key.\n- **ORC-014: not applicable.** No controlled provider or host supplies a\n  premise.\n\n## Limits\n\nA truncate committed inside the sync function itself, whose ready callback\nthrew, threw from `commit()` and moved the Collection to `error`. The\n[follow-up record](2026-10-05-round-3-followups.md) defers that error the way\n`ops.markReady()` does.\n\nThe grid covers one optimistic callback write per truncate. These histories\nremain outside it:\n\n- a callback that rolls back an existing request;\n- a non-optimistic callback write;\n- several callbacks that write;\n- generated histories that combine ready callbacks with the optimistic-history\n  grammar.\n\n## Note added 2026-10-05: settlement drop\n\nThis fix merged with the settlement-drop change\n([2026-10-03 review](2026-10-03-settlement-drop.md)), where readiness counts\naccepted rows. A ready truncate batch still publishes before ready effects\nrun: the reentrancy oracle passes on the merged revision, and S6 still fails\nit.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Ready-callback writes during a truncate\n\nBase revision: `931e8346f` (`main`). Grid revision: `4c338ddbd`. First\nrepair revision: `79c7786b4` (deferral). Split-transition revision:\n`9bbeb1063`.\n\n## Law\n\nEach change message must be valid for a consumer that has applied every\nearlier message: an insert names an absent key, and an update or delete names\na present one. Authority: the change-message contract in\n`packages/db/tests/change-event-history-oracle.test.ts` and issue #1901.\n\nA truncate commit that makes the Collection ready runs ready callbacks\n(`onFirstReady` and `status:change` listeners) during the commit, and a\ncallback may write. The truncate's messages and the callback's messages\ntogether must be valid for every subscriber, with or without initial state and\nwith or without a filter. Each subscriber must end at the Collection's rows.\nSubscribers receive the truncate's messages only after the Collection is ready.\n\n## Gap and bug\n\nState mutation round 3 found that a mutant which ran `markReady` before the\ntruncate reapply survived the suite. The ready-callback witness covered only an\nedit of a replaced key, through a subscriber with initial state, whose sent-key\nfilter hides a repeated message.\n\nThe bug on `main`: the truncate built its batch from the replaced rows, then\ncalled `markReady` before it published. A callback's messages describe the\nreplaced rows, but they reached subscribers before the batch that moves\nsubscribers to those rows. Reachable failures:\n\n- A callback deletes a replaced key. The callback publishes the delete, and\n  then the prefix deletes the key again.\n- A callback deletes a key that only the replacement holds. The subscriber\n  gets a delete for a row it never held.\n- A callback changes a key that has an active prior request. The batch's\n  re-applied insert still carries the prior value.\n- A filtered subscriber holds the pre-truncate row. The callback's delete\n  carries the replaced value, so the filter drops it, and the subscriber keeps\n  a row that the Collection removed.\n\nThe first repair (`7331f25aa`) dropped prefix deletes for keys a callback\ndeleted. Code review and CodeRabbit showed the other three failures. That\nrepair also made the stale re-applied row silent, because the repeated delete\nno longer exposed it.\n\n## Repair and witnesses\n\n- The ready transition takes a step that runs after the status reads `ready`\n  and before status listeners, ready callbacks, and the empty ready event. A\n  truncate that makes the Collection ready emits its batch in that step. So\n  the batch is built and enriched before any callback runs, subscribers\n  receive it while the Collection is ready, and every ready effect follows it.\n- An earlier revision held the batch in a publication deferral around\n  `markReady`. A high-effort review found side effects of that design, listed\n  below, and the maintainer chose to split the ready transition instead.\n- The grid in `collection-sync-reentrancy-oracle.test.ts` derives expected\n  rows from a model that overlays active intents, in order, on the source rows.\n  It crosses two hooks, four prior requests, every legal callback write to keys\n  1 through 4, and three subscribers. Before the truncate the source holds keys\n  1 and 4. The replacement holds 1 and 2, so key 4's prefix delete must remain.\n- Each grid case also checks three observations. A message that carries a\n  source row is synced and remote unless a prior request owns its key. A live\n  query over the Collection becomes ready showing replaced rows. A raw\n  subscriber that the ready callback creates receives messages that are valid\n  for the rows it saw.\n\n## High-effort review of the deferral\n\n| Item | Finding | Outcome |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | The deferral enriched the batch at publication, so a source row could go out as local. | Fixed by the split. 48 grid cases fail with the deferral. |\n| 2 | The empty ready event skipped the deferral, so a live query became ready with pre-truncate rows. | Fixed by the split. All 144 cases fail with the deferral. |\n| 3 | A truncate committed inside the sync function, with a throwing `onFirstReady`, throws from `commit()` and moves the Collection to `error`. `ops.markReady()` defers the same error. | Confirmed on `main` too. Open, for a separate fix. |\n| 4 | A subscriber that a ready callback creates received the whole batch. | Fixed by the split. 120 cases fail with the deferral. |\n| 5 | The deferral delivered the batch and the callbacks' messages as one uncomposed batch. | Fixed by the split: they are separate publications. |\n| 6 | The deferral is a truncate-only special case. Split the ready transition. | Adopted. |\n| 7 | One failure-fidelity case was vacuous. | Fixed: an already-ready Collection runs no ready callbacks, so the case is removed. |\n| 8 | The grid did not check virtual props, live queries, or callback subscribers. | Fixed: see the witnesses above. |\n| 9 | The two guards for the deferral encoded one condition. | Gone with the deferral. |\n| 10 | A cleanup inside a ready listener dropped the deferred batch. | Fixed by the split: the batch emits before listeners run. |\n\n## Failure fidelity\n\nCodeRabbit found that the deferral let a subscriber error escape from the\npublication before the commit resolved its applied receipts. A probe showed the\nsame hang on `main`, through `markReady`'s empty ready event. When a truncate\nmade the Collection ready, a throwing subscriber or `onFirstReady` callback left\na held receipt pending forever.\n\nThe emit and the ready transition now run through one capture that keeps the\nfirst error. The commit reports that error after its receipts settle, as it\ndoes when the Collection is already ready. A witness holds a sync transaction\nbehind a persisting request, then truncates while a subscriber or a ready\ncallback throws. A third case throws from a subscriber on an already-ready\nCollection. The two not-yet-ready cases fail on `main` and on the uncaptured\ndeferral.\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\n- **ORC-001: met.** The law above names its authority and the subscriber\n  modes it covers.\n- **ORC-002: met.** The overlay model reads no production state. The checker\n  validates each message against the subscriber's own replica.\n- **ORC-003: met.** The prose above the grid states the law, the model, and\n  why keys 2 and 4 are in the grid.\n- **ORC-004: met.** The enumeration control checks the case count, five named\n  witnesses, and two excluded illegal writes.\n- **ORC-005: met.** Each case writes through a real Collection's sync and\n  mutation APIs. It observes `subscribeChanges` batches, `collection.status`\n  at delivery, and `collection.state`.\n- **ORC-006: met.**\n  - `main` and the first repair each fail 54 of 144 cases.\n  - A variant that marks the Collection ready after emitting fails 122 cases,\n    because subscribers then receive the batch while the Collection is loading.\n  - The publication deferral fails all 144 cases on the live-query check, 120\n    on the callback-subscriber check, and 48 on the virtual-props check.\n- **ORC-007: not applicable.** The grid is a fixed enumeration.\n- **ORC-008: not applicable.** No stateful model changed.\n- **ORC-009: met.** \"Ready callback\" means an `onFirstReady` callback or a\n  `status:change` listener for `ready`.\n- **ORC-010: met.** Each case unsubscribes, settles its requests, and cleans\n  up the Collection in a `finally` block. The failure-fidelity witness checks\n  that a thrown subscriber or callback error is reported after every receipt\n  settles.\n- **ORC-011: not applicable.** No reviewer named a fault shared by production\n  and the model.\n- **ORC-012: met by this record.**\n- **ORC-013: met.** Each callback write appears beside its neighbors: the same\n  key with and without a prior request, and a replaced key beside a\n  replacement-only key and an omitted key.\n- **ORC-014: not applicable.** No controlled provider or host supplies a\n  premise.\n\n## Limits\n\nA truncate committed inside the sync function itself, whose ready callback\nthrew, threw from `commit()` and moved the Collection to `error`. The\n[follow-up record](2026-10-05-round-3-followups.md) defers that error the way\n`ops.markReady()` does.\n\nThe grid covers one optimistic callback write per truncate. These histories\nremain outside it:\n\n- a callback that rolls back an existing request;\n- a non-optimistic callback write;\n- several callbacks that write;\n- generated histories that combine ready callbacks with the optimistic-history\n  grammar.\n\n## Note added 2026-10-05: settlement drop\n\nThis fix merged with the settlement-drop change\n([2026-10-03 review](2026-10-03-settlement-drop.md)), where readiness counts\naccepted rows. A ready truncate batch still publishes before ready effects\nrun: the reentrancy oracle passes on the merged revision, and S6 still fails\nit.\n"}
